Date: Tue, 27 Apr 93 23:52:50 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: META: A Cooling Off Period?

Folks, things are really getting out of hand. I suggest a cooling-off
period is in order.

Though there's still a week or so to run in my self-imposed "vacation" from
posting to this list (though I posted the urgent material related to the
Clipper, of course, and I slipped up a couple of time and posted some minor
replies), I simply have to comment on the name-calling, debates about the
most fundamental issues, and general spirit of acrimony on this list.

Please, folks, we wouldn't be on this list if we didn't share some basic
values and some basic interests in the future! Calling people fools,
bigots, Communists, Randroids, etc., does not seem to be at all useful.

This is from my perspective as an avid reader of the list and, perhaps soon
again, an avid poster. I'm not guiltless, either. I admit I've used biting
humor at times, and for this I'm sorry.

But I would hate to see this list destroy itself in an orgy of screams and
shouts, insults, ad hominem attacks, and appeals to emotion rather than
reason.

The "Environment" is a tough issue, no matter what your politics are. Years
of debate and tens of thousands of pages of argument have not resulted in
any consensus--this is not to say there is no solution, no best strategy,
just that it's not "obvious" in the same way that some other things are
obvious. Most libertarians I know are as conflicted about what to do as the
rest of the ideological spectrum is. And debating nuclear power is
generally not productive, I've found. (It can be, but not through the kind
of "sound bites" which our list necessitates....the issues just can't be
adequately addressed, explained, numbers worked out, etc.).

Likewise, who cares whether Earth Day was Lenin's birthday? And why so much
acrimony? What to do about the environment is a legitimate issue, of
course, but some of the so-called debate here is purely ad hominem.

(Hypertext-like digression: I at first dismissed Krzys' comments (though
not in print, of course), but then I checked and discovered Lenin was
indeed born on 22 April 1870 (new calendar)...the first Earth Day was
exactly 100 years later, on 22 April 1970. Perhaps there is something to
this, I thought to myself. On the other hand, the environmental movement
was building by 1969-70 and _some_ day had to be picked, so the 100 years
part is perhaps accidental. There's also the chance that someone later
noticed the date and this is just a kind of "urban legend." I tried to find
some history of Earth Day, but couldn't find anything beyond the usual
simple facts and figures. My suspicion is that only the organizers know for
sure--perhaps Krzys' read about it in their memoirs. It would help the
debate--and might've squelched the acrimony--if Krzys' could've at least
*hinted* at the source of his information.)

We're not going to resolve anything by arguing about labels, about whether
Greens are Communists, or about the well-trodden ground of nuclear power.
(I noted with interest at the Jerome Freedman party that Max More's friend
Nancy is an official (PR, I think) with the Green Party of Los Angeles. She
was pleasant and her Green connections were an interesting topic for
discussion. I talked to her a bit about how nuclear's gotten a bad rap and
she talked to me about Aspen and the problems L.A. faces.)

And then there's the Randroid flame war. I would think that debating
Objectivism and Rand is a total waste of time and will only lead to angry
words. 

(Another hypertext-like digression: As it turns out, I read "Atlas
Shrugged" in 2 intense days in 1968, at the age of 16, and can honestly say
it utterly and completely changed my life. I was already a Heinlein fan, so
the philosophy and espousal of free market ideas was not novel ("A.S." was
"not (a) novel" in other ways, too!). But the "propaganda value" of it and
"The Fountainhead" (which I read a few days later) was immense. A few years
later, in around 1970-71, all of my roomates at college had read Rand, all
were influenced immensely, but all of us were fairly skeptical of the "late
Rand" extremist Objectivism. And in 1972 I cast my first vote in a
Presidential election for John Hospers of the just-formed Libertarian
Party. I continued to recommend "A.S." to others, especially girlfriends
(so they could see what makes me tick and not be shocked to hear me defend
capitalism), but I've been unable these last 25 years to sit down and
reread it. I've tried several times. I guess I had to read it in one
cathartic blast.)

So, debates about the "basics" of ideology seem doomed. I hope my absence
these last few weeks, except for the Clipper postings and the lapses I
mentioned, has earned me at least a *slight* amount of slack in suggesting
we all try to cool it on these emotional issues.

There are too many important issues to work on. The future awaits. Wasting
our time on witchunts is a bad idea (besides, my report, "Paganism and
Science: The Hammer of Witches" will light a fire under this witch issue
soon enough :-} ).

Sometimes when someone just obviously doesn't "get it," instead of beating
them up some more, with more posts, more tirades, I just leave it. If they
just don't get it, then this state of ignorance is their own punishment. I
call this a mixture of Harry Browne and Calvinism. (That's also the reason
I rarely put smileys in my attempts at humor or explain myself beyond what
I've already said.)

Anyway, I hope no one thinks I'm lecturing or being high-and-mighty. I just
don't want to see this list destroy itself in entropy-increasing flames. I
want there to be something left when I return!

Cheers!

-Tim May
