From: davisd@pierce.ee.washington.edu
Subject: RAND: Warning: Objectivism may be hazardous to your health (was MONEY: Objectivists and Gold) 

I disagree with both Ed and Perry about this.

Ed, I don't think you should worry much about the growth of
Objectivist discussion groups. Objectivists make pretty good
neighbors, and the Randroids are probably made better neighbors by
Objectivism, although you probably won't be asking them in for tea too
often.

If I recall correctly, you thought reading Rand would turn people into
antisocial misfits. I agree that some people running around
calling themselves Objectivists are spiteful obnoxious jerks. I
maintain that most of those were that way before they read Rand. It
makes some sense. People who hate others and find Rand will 
take her philosophy as a justification for their hatred, making the
same mistake that people who oppose any form of egoism do, mistaking
valuing yourself with hatred of others. 

In my own case, reading Rand led me to value others much more than I
had before. Instead of having some inexplicable duty to love my
family, which I had always felt to be false, I honestly examined what
values their lives held for me. I found quite a lot. Treating people
according to their value to me has made me much more sociable than I
used to be. I'm practically the social butterfly of my department.
(Well, it is the electrical engineering department, so there isn't a
hell of a lot of competition.)

By the by, I am neither a big or little O objectivist. I don't agree
with Rand on epistemology or ethics, although I do find a lot of value
in Rand.

Which brings me to Perry's description of Rand as a bad writer. I've
seen Perry make this claim before. I cannot help but wander by what
standard Perry judges Rand's writing. I find a lot of value in Rand's
novels. I call novels that I value highly good novels, and the people
who write them, good writers. Specifically, I have yet to find any
other writer who presents a better sense of life than Rand. I can
conceive of it as a possibility, because she has her own problems in
this area, but she is still #1 in my book. 

Perry, since you say that Rand is a bad writer, I will assume that you
have a list of at least a dozen writers who present a better sense of
life in their novels than Rand does, by your own standards of sense of
life. I would greatly appreciate it if you would share that list.

