Date: Wed, 28 Apr 93 09:16:53 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: EPIST: Why do formal systems work?

As a student of the history of astronomy, I feel compelled to comment
on the subject of Kepler.  Before Tycho, the errors in the assumed
postions of the stars, and hence the errors in the measured postions
of the planets, was on the order of 10 minutes of arc.  For scale,
the resolving power of the human eye is about 1 minute of arc,
and the Moon is about 30 minutes of arc across.  The errors in
the epicyclic theory could be made as low as 8 minutes of arc
in longitude for Mars.  Mars is the 'hard case', being the nearest
planet with a large eccentricity.  Venus has an eccentricity of
0.007 while Mars has an eccentricity of 0.093.  Thus, while Venus
is slightly closer to Earth, it's orbit can actually be represented
quite well by a circle.

While the longitude of Mars could be brought down to within pre-
Tycho observation errors, the latitude was considered a 'hopeless
problem'.  The Ptolemaic system assumed the Earth was the center of
motion.  In reality, Mars has a 1.8 degree inclination to the
plane of the Earth's orbit, but centered on the Sun.  At opposition,
when Mars is opposite the Sun (i.e. the Earth is directly inside
Mars), the distance is about 0.4 astronomical units, so the latitude
of Mars at opposition would vary by 4.5 degrees, and so theory
with the Earth at the center gave any kind of good answer.

Tycho built larger and more accurate instruments for measuring the
positions of things in the sky. His measurement errors were about
2 minutes of arc, which is about as good as can be expected before
the introduction of the telescope, and was pushing the limits of
the human eye.  Kepler inherited vast piles of observations made
by Tycho.  He eventually, after trying many possibilities, found
that an ellipse with the Sun at one focus, and the speed of the
planet varying inversely with distance from the Sun, matched the
observations of Mars' longitude at all points in it's orbit.

Then by solving for an inclination and ascending node, he found he
could match the observations of latitude as well.  Messr's Forste
and Hammar are both correct in their previous messages.  In longitude
only the improved observations of Tycho exposed the errors of
the Ptolemaic system, while in latitude the old system was in
gross error, and Kepler reduced the error by orders of magnitude.

Dani Eder
(former court astrologer to the Kingdom of Meridies)


