Date: Wed, 28 Apr 1993 10:37:32 -0400
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: RAND: Warning: Objectivism may be hazardous to your health (was MONEY: Objectivists and Gold) 
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

This is long and detailed -- I spent some time answering the question
"why is Rand a bad writer", and I've illustrated it with a comparitive
quote.

davisd@pierce.ee.washington.edu says, after saying nice things about
Rand with which I agree:

> Which brings me to Perry's description of Rand as a bad writer. I've
> seen Perry make this claim before. I cannot help but wander by what
> standard Perry judges Rand's writing.

She's no Gabriel Garcia Marquez, even though his politics stink.
She's no Mark Helprin.
She's no Thomas Hardy.
She's no Ernest Hemingway.
She's no William Shakespeare

(Pardon my naming so many male writers. They were some of the first
that came to mind. Mea culpa, mea culpa, mea maxima culpa. She's no
George Sand or Emily Bronte either.)

I'm afraid that I have the misfortune of being equally interested in
literature and science -- I had the good fortune of going to Columbia,
where the core cirriculum forced me to explore both venues. I read
literature voraciously, and permit me to say, Ms. Rand is perhaps the
worst author I am willing to read bar none.

Her characters are wooden. She engages in the cardinal sin of the
expository lump on a virtually continuous basis. Her plots are often
"idiot plots", meaning if someone wasn't an idiot the plot wouldn't
progress. ("If Dagny had only realized her folly by page 200, we could
have been spared the next 800 pages!") -- don't tell me "idiot plots"
are a needed literary device, because they are not -- skilled writers
manage without them.  She has an obsession with rape.  She writes
horrible prose with revolting sentence structure overladen with
adjectives. What she calls "romantic fiction" seems to me to mean "I
can't write about real humans, and I use meaningless adjectives".

I also love reading her books -- they are filled with great ideas.
But, sad to say, even I write better fiction than her, and when I used
to stand up during the literary exercise at the Philolexian society to
read my prose people would often search in vein for instruments with
which to terminate their own lives.

> I find a lot of value in Rand's
> novels.

So do I. They are well worth reading for the ideas. However, she is an
excruciatingly bad writer.

Allow me to demonstrate. Here are two passages with nature
descriptions that both attempt to paint a natural scene with words.

  "Howard Roark laughed.
   He stood naked at the edge of a cliff. The lake lay far below him. A
 frozen explosion of granite burst in flight to the sky over motionless
 water. The water seemed immovable, the stone -- flowing. The stone had
 the stillness of one brief moment in battle when thrust meets thrust
 and the currents are held in a pause more dynamic than motion. The
 stone glowed, wet with sunrays.
   The lake below was only a thin steel ring that cut rocks in half. The
 rocks went on into the depth, unchanged. They began and ended in the
 sky. So that the world seemed susspended in space, an island floating
 on nothing, anchored to the feet of the man on the cliff."

That was Ayn Rand.

  "No, he had never written about Paris. Not the Paris he cared about.
   But what about the rest that he had never written?
 What about the ranch and the silvered gray of the sage brush, the
 quick, clear water in the irrigation ditches, and the heavy green of
 the alfalfa. The trail went up into the hills and the cattle in the
 summer were as shy as deer. The bawling and the steady noise and slow
 moving mass raising a dust as you brought them down in the fall. And
 behind the mountains, the clear sharpness of the peak in the evening
 light and, riding down the trail in the moonlight, bright across the
 valey. Now he remembered coming down through the timber in the dark
 holding the horse's tail when you could not see and all the stories
 that he meant to write."

That was Ernest Hemingway.

I am perhaps excessively critical, having been trained to spot the
difference, but I'm afraid that in my own subjective opinion Rand is
attempting to poetically describe a scene, and is making a complete
hash of it. Flowing stones? "A pause more dynamic than motion"? Feh.
Tolstoy would have had her shot, and he was a radical pacifist. She's
writing the way a teenager does, shouting "look at me! I can use fancy
descriptions like the ones in those books I read", only she doesn't
grok what makes those descriptions work or fail.

Hemingway makes his points with no verbiage, no purple prose, no
meaningless descriptions thrown in to make you think the writer is
