Subject: Earth Day

I feel the need to comment on the opinion of some that property rights
over a piece of land extend high up into the air.  My understanding of
property law is that a thing must be capable of exclusive posession and
control to become an object of property.  Thus the volume above a
tract of land can be part of the property to the extent that you can
excercise control over it.  For example, you can build a tower or
fly an aerostat (a tethered baloon) high in the air.

By physical presence, you can control the occupancy of the airspace
above the land.  The same reasoning, however, does not apply to
the air itself.  Without control of the winds, which is generally
beyond technical capabilities today, you cannot assert control over
any particular set of air molecules.  Hence you cannot be said to
own that air.

Note the distinction between the volume of space above a piece of
land, and the air which fills that volume.

Now, if something falls down and contaminates the land itself,
or blocks the sun from shining on the land, that is a different
matter, for that affects the land itself, which is actually
owned.

Dani Eder
 

