Subject: Radar Detectors

Ahh.. Another topic of interest to me - civilian applications of
Stealth technology.  If you want to reduce your exposure to 
speeding tickets, you have to reduce the signal reflected back
to their gun and/or supply a signal that you want them to get.

Your typical car is reflecting the police radar signal back
from a number of metal surfaces that are more or less perpendicular
to the gun.  These include the radiator grille, the leading edge
of the wheel hub (through the tire), and pieces of chrome trim.

To reduce your radar signature, you want the reflection planes
off your vehicle to be off in directions away from the radar gun.
As a simple example, if the front of your car were a flat mirror
inclined 20 degrees from horizontal (leading edge close to the
ground), all the microwaves would be reflected up into the air,
and not back to the radar gun.  For most people, this is too
big a modification, but there are some things you can do.

Choose tires with kevlar rather than steel belting.  Re-mount
the radiator tilted back.  Redo trim areas that provide a
vertical reflection surface to be angled.

Now, for active signal control, what you want is a corner reflector,
a tried and true way of getting an enhanced signal reflection.

Why do you want to do that?, well you can control the refelcted signal.
Imagine a reflector made of sheet metal bent at a 90 degree angle,
a few inches wide by a few feet long.  This reflects radar signals
really well.  Now take a set of these and mount them on a horizontal
axis like the paddles of a paddle-wheel boat.  Spin on the horizontal
axis so the side of the reflector which reflects is moving backward
relative to the vehicle motion.  What the radar gun should see
is reflections mostly off the corner reflector, which is moving
slower relative to the ground than the vehicle is.  So the
speed reading will be lower.

Dani Eder

